From: Circle OS Build <ops@circleos.org>
Date: 2026-05-30
Subject: [PATCH 0002/000X] manifest: promote INTERNET from normal to dangerous

INTERNET ships as android:protectionLevel="normal|instant" in upstream
AOSP, which auto-grants it at install. Circle OS treats outbound network
access as a user decision, not an app's claim, so we demote it to a
runtime-grantable permission by changing the protectionLevel to
"dangerous|instant".

Consequence: at first install, third-party packages will not have
INTERNET granted. The CirclePrivacyService binder (see vendor/circle/
privacy/) is the source of truth for whether the user has granted it.
Until the fake-response provider lands, denied apps will see
SecurityException on socket open --- this is intentional for the
intermediate state.

The 'instant' flag is preserved so instant apps continue to behave
as upstream specifies.

Targets: AOSP 15, tag android-15.0.0_r20.
Touches: core/res/AndroidManifest.xml

diff --git a/core/res/AndroidManifest.xml b/core/res/AndroidManifest.xml
index 6b8056c77fda..73ce4d882fa6 100644
--- a/core/res/AndroidManifest.xml
+++ b/core/res/AndroidManifest.xml
@@ -2176,12 +2176,16 @@
     <eat-comment />
 
     <!-- Allows applications to open network sockets.
-         <p>Protection level: normal
+         <p>Protection level: dangerous
+         <p>Circle OS modification: promoted from normal to dangerous so that
+            outbound network access is granted per-app at runtime via the
+            Circle Privacy Framework (CirclePrivacyService). The user is the
+            authority on which apps may reach the network.
     -->
     <permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET"
         android:description="@string/permdesc_createNetworkSockets"
         android:label="@string/permlab_createNetworkSockets"
-        android:protectionLevel="normal|instant" />
+        android:protectionLevel="dangerous|instant" />
 
     <!-- Allows applications to access information about networks.
          <p>Protection level: normal
